I've tried everything I can think of: setting MAIL, MAILER, MAILBOX,
MAILREADER, etc., but no matter what I do, no mail demons appear to hand
me a scroll of mail when mail arrives in my mailbox!  Are the game
designers under the misapprehension that only UNIX systems have mail? 
Considering the number of PCs and LANs out there, there may actually be
mail on *more* DOS and OS/2 systems (and maybe Macs) than UNIX systems! 
Certainly there's plenty!

I've looked at the code for mail.c, and I find it rather opaque.  I'd be
glad to volunteer to help add mail interfaces for other platforms (at
least those I have access to), but I may need some help deciphering the
logic involved in the current code.

On a separate, unrelated question, why does the "core dumped" message
when you eat an apple only appear on UNIX versions?  First of all, my
OS/2 compiler (gcc) supports core dumps (among other reasons, to allow
EMACS and PERL executables to be built from dumps).  Second of all,
while the message may not invoke the terror under, say, DOS or
Macintosh, that it does under a UNIX version, it can still evoke a
giggle from those who know and recognize the message.  Many people, even
those who have never used UNIX, know that (Net)hack was originally a
UNIX game, and may well get the joke.
